package Rendering

use Libraries.Containers.Array
use Libraries.Interface.Item3D
use Libraries.Game.BoundingBox
use Libraries.Game.Graphics.Camera
use Libraries.Game.Graphics.PointLight
use Libraries.Game.Graphics.DirectionalLight
use Libraries.Compute.Matrix4
use Libraries.Compute.Vector3
use Libraries.Compute.Math

/*
RenderTree3D keeps track of where every drawable item in a 3D scene is, so
that drawing and shadows only handle the items that matter. It answers these
questions:

- Which items can the camera see? (CollectVisible)
- Which items could cast a shadow into one cascade of a directional light's
  shadows? (CollectDirectionalShadowCasters)
- Which items are within a point light's reach? (CollectPointShadowCasters)
- Which items overlap a box or a sphere? (CollectInBox, CollectInSphere)

This class only describes what a render tree does. Its actions are blank.
Use one of the kinds of render tree, which can be swapped for one another
because they all have these actions:

- DynamicVolumeRenderTree, a tree of boxes that fit around the items, like
  the one physics uses. A good choice for most scenes, and for scenes whose
  items are spread unevenly or move a lot.
- QuadTree3D, which divides the ground into squares. A good choice for a
  scene that is mostly flat and spread out, such as terrain and the things
  on it.
- Octree, which divides space into cubes. A good choice for a scene spread
  out in every direction, such as space or a tall city.

Each answer is added to an Array the caller passes in. The caller empties the
array when it starts a new frame or pass, with Empty(false) so the array
keeps its storage and no new one is made. The answers include hidden items;
the caller can leave them out with IsShowing or IsShowingWithParents.

A render tree does not watch items for changes. Whoever owns it, such as
Layer3D, calls Moved for each item whose position, rotation or scale has
changed, once per frame before drawing, and EndFrame after drawing.

Only items with bounds can be added. An item whose bounds cannot be found,
such as one that draws itself, is not added, and Add returns false. Its owner
draws it every frame, without asking the tree.

The few actions here that are not blank are the same for every kind of
tree: the Add and CollectVisible actions that fill in a missing value and
pass the request on, IsShowing and IsShowingWithParents, and the private
helpers that find an item's bounds.
*/
class RenderTree3D
    Vector3 minimum
    Vector3 maximum
    Math math

    /*
    This action adds an item that is not expected to move.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to add.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item was added. False if it was already in
    the tree, or has no bounds the tree can find.
    */
    action Add(Item3D item) returns boolean
        return Add(item, true)
    end

    /*
    This action adds an item, using bounds the tree finds for it: a box of
    the item's width, height and depth, centered on the item. For an item
    whose shape is not centered on it, such as a model whose origin is at
    its feet, use the Add action that takes bounds instead.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to add.
    Attribute: Parameter isStatic True if the item is not expected to move.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item was added. False if it was already in
    the tree, or has no bounds the tree can find.
    */
    action Add(Item3D item, boolean isStatic) returns boolean
        if item = undefined or Has(item)
            return false
        end
        BoundingBox local = GetBounds(item)
        if local = undefined
            return false
        end
        return Add(item, local, isStatic)
    end

    /*
    This action adds an item that is not expected to move, with the given
    bounds in its own coordinates.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to add.
    Attribute: Parameter localBounds The item's bounds in its own coordinates.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item was added, false if it was already in
    the tree.
    */
    action Add(Item3D item, BoundingBox localBounds) returns boolean
        return Add(item, localBounds, true)
    end

    /*
    This action adds an item with the given bounds in its own coordinates.
    Use it for items whose bounds the tree cannot find, such as terrain
    chunks. A terrain chunk should give bounds that hold every level of
    detail, so changing the level does not change the tree.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to add.
    Attribute: Parameter localBounds The item's bounds in its own coordinates.
    Attribute: Parameter isStatic True if the item is not expected to move.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item was added, false if it was already in
    the tree.
    */
    action Add(Item3D item, BoundingBox localBounds, boolean isStatic) returns boolean
        return false
    end

    /*
    This action removes an item. If the item is a point light, anything the
    tree remembered for it is also forgotten.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to remove.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item was in the tree.
    */
    action Remove(Item3D item) returns boolean
        return false
    end

    /*
    This action removes every item.
    */
    action Empty
    end

    /*
    This action returns true if the item is in the tree.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to look for.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item is in the tree.
    */
    action Has(Item3D item) returns boolean
        return false
    end

    /*
    This action returns the leaf that holds an item, or undefined if the
    item is not in the tree. Finding it means a lookup in a hash table, so
    an owner that tells the tree about moves every frame should get each
    item's leaf once, after adding the item, keep it, and pass it to
    Moved(leaf) instead of the item. The leaf must not be used after the
    item is removed, and its setters must not be called from outside the
    tree.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item to find.
    Attribute: Returns The item's leaf, or undefined if it is not in the tree.
    */
    action GetLeaf(Item3D item) returns RenderTreeNode3D
        return undefined
    end

    /*
    This action tells the tree an item's position, rotation or scale has
    changed. It looks up the item's leaf each time. Moved(leaf) does not.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item that moved.
    */
    action Moved(Item3D item)
    end

    /*
    This action does the same as Moved(item), for the leaf GetLeaf returned
    for that item, without looking it up. A leaf whose item has been
    removed is ignored.

    Attribute: Parameter leaf The leaf GetLeaf returned for the item.
    */
    action Moved(RenderTreeNode3D leaf)
    end

    /*
    This action changes an item's bounds in its own coordinates, for example
    when its mesh or its level of detail changes.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item whose bounds changed.
    Attribute: Parameter localBounds The item's new bounds in its own
    coordinates.
    */
    action SetLocalBounds(Item3D item, BoundingBox localBounds)
    end

    /*
    This action ends a frame. Call it once per frame, after drawing.
    */
    action EndFrame
    end

    /*
    This action adds the items the camera can see to the results.

    Attribute: Parameter camera The camera.
    Attribute: Parameter results The array to add the items to.
    */
    action CollectVisible(Camera camera, Array<Item3D> results)
        CollectVisible(camera:GetCombinedMatrix(), results)
    end

    /*
    This action adds the items inside the view of the given view-projection
    matrix to the results.

    Attribute: Parameter viewProjection The view-projection matrix.
    Attribute: Parameter results The array to add the items to.
    */
    action CollectVisible(Matrix4 viewProjection, Array<Item3D> results)
    end

    /*
    This action adds the items that could cast a shadow into one cascade of
    a directional light's shadows to the results. Each cascade covers part
    of the camera's view and is drawn in its own pass, so this is asked once
    per cascade.

    The cascade's view-projection matrix comes from the light's shadow data,
    which EnvironmentVulkan updates each frame, so ask after it has. A light
    without shadow data for the cascade, such as in OpenGL, adds nothing.

    Attribute: Parameter light The directional light.
    Attribute: Parameter cascade Which cascade, counting from 0.
    Attribute: Parameter results The array to add the items to.
    */
    action CollectDirectionalShadowCasters(DirectionalLight light, integer cascade, Array<Item3D> results)
    end

    /*
    This action adds the items within a point light's reach, as given by its
    GetRange action, to the results.

    Attribute: Parameter light The point light.
    Attribute: Parameter results The array to add the items to.
    */
    action CollectPointShadowCasters(PointLight light, Array<Item3D> results)
    end

    /*
    This action adds the items overlapping a box to the results.

    Attribute: Parameter box The box, in world coordinates.
    Attribute: Parameter results The array to add the items to.
    */
    action CollectInBox(BoundingBox box, Array<Item3D> results)
    end

    /*
    This action adds the items overlapping a sphere to the results.

    Attribute: Parameter x The x coordinate of the sphere's center.
    Attribute: Parameter y The y coordinate of the sphere's center.
    Attribute: Parameter z The z coordinate of the sphere's center.
    Attribute: Parameter radius The sphere's radius.
    Attribute: Parameter results The array to add the items to.
    */
    action CollectInSphere(number x, number y, number z, number radius, Array<Item3D> results)
    end

    /*
    This action returns true if the item is showing. It does not look at
    the item's parents, so an item whose parent is hidden still counts as
    showing.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item is showing.
    */
    action IsShowing(Item3D item) returns boolean
        return item:IsShowing()
    end

    /*
    This action returns true if the item and all of its parents are showing.
    Layer3D draws by walking down from each item, so a hidden parent hides
    its children. The tree holds items on their own, so this checks upward
    instead.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item and all of its parents are showing.
    */
    action IsShowingWithParents(Item3D item) returns boolean
        Item3D current = item
        repeat while current not= undefined
            if not current:IsShowing()
                return false
            end
            current = current:GetParent()
        end
        return true
    end

    /*
    This action returns how many items are in the tree.

    Attribute: Returns The number of items.
    */
    action GetSize returns integer
        return 0
    end

    /*
    This action returns how many items are in the static part of the tree,
    which holds items that are not moving.

    Attribute: Returns The number of static items.
    */
    action GetStaticSize returns integer
        return 0
    end

    /*
    This action returns how many items are in the dynamic part of the tree,
    which holds items that are moving.

    Attribute: Returns The number of dynamic items.
    */
    action GetDynamicSize returns integer
        return 0
    end

    /*
    This action returns true if the item is in the static part of the tree,
    and false if it is in the dynamic part or not in the tree.

    Attribute: Parameter item The item.
    Attribute: Returns True if the item is static.
    */
    action IsStatic(Item3D item) returns boolean
        return false
    end

    /*
    This action returns how many levels the static part has below its top,
    or -1 when it is empty. What a level is depends on the kind of tree.

    Attribute: Returns The number of levels.
    */
    action GetStaticHeight returns integer
        return -1
    end

    /*
    This action returns how many levels the dynamic part has below its top,
    or -1 when it is empty.

    Attribute: Returns The number of levels.
    */
    action GetDynamicHeight returns integer
        return -1
    end

    /*
    This action returns a number that goes up by one every time the static
    part changes. Items in the static part never move, so anything worked
    out from them stays correct until this number changes.

    Use it to keep work that depends only on static items, such as a list of
    the static items within a light's reach, or data prepared for drawing
    static items. Save the number when doing the work, and redo the work
    only when GetStaticVersion returns a different number.

    Attribute: Returns The static version.
    */
    action GetStaticVersion returns integer
        return 0
    end

    /*
    This action returns an item's bounds in its own coordinates, or undefined
    if it has none the tree can find.

    They are a box of the item's width, height and depth, centered on the
    item. A model gets its width, height and depth from its blueprint when
    it is loaded, so this works for models too. An item with no size has no
    bounds. A negative size, which a blueprint with a bug in its measuring
    could give, is also treated as no bounds. Flat bounds, such as a plane's,
    are allowed, though BoundingBox:IsValid would reject them.
    */
    private action GetBounds(Item3D item) returns BoundingBox
        number halfWidth = item:GetWidth() / 2
        number halfHeight = item:GetHeight() / 2
        number halfDepth = item:GetDepth() / 2
        if halfWidth < 0 or halfHeight < 0 or halfDepth < 0
            return undefined
        end
        if halfWidth = 0 and halfHeight = 0 and halfDepth = 0
            return undefined
        end
        BoundingBox box
        minimum:Set(-halfWidth, -halfHeight, -halfDepth)
        maximum:Set(halfWidth, halfHeight, halfDepth)
        box:Set(minimum, maximum)
        return box
    end

    /*
    This action puts a leaf's item's bounds in world coordinates into the
    given box.

    The item's bounds in its own coordinates are a center and half sizes.
    The world center is the item's transform applied to the center. The
    world half size on each axis is the sum of the absolute values of the
    transform's entries in that row, each times the matching half size. This
    is the smallest axis-aligned box around the turned box, and needs no
    corners. The transform includes the item's scale and its parent's offset.
    */
    private action CalculateWorldBounds(RenderTreeNode3D leaf, BoundingBox out)
        Matrix4 transform = leaf:GetItem():GetTransform()
        number cx = leaf:GetLocalCenterX()
        number cy = leaf:GetLocalCenterY()
        number cz = leaf:GetLocalCenterZ()
        number hx = leaf:GetLocalHalfX()
        number hy = leaf:GetLocalHalfY()
        number hz = leaf:GetLocalHalfZ()

        number worldX = transform:row0column0 * cx + transform:row0column1 * cy + transform:row0column2 * cz + transform:row0column3
        number worldY = transform:row1column0 * cx + transform:row1column1 * cy + transform:row1column2 * cz + transform:row1column3
        number worldZ = transform:row2column0 * cx + transform:row2column1 * cy + transform:row2column2 * cz + transform:row2column3

        number halfX = math:AbsoluteValue(transform:row0column0) * hx + math:AbsoluteValue(transform:row0column1) * hy +
            math:AbsoluteValue(transform:row0column2) * hz
        number halfY = math:AbsoluteValue(transform:row1column0) * hx + math:AbsoluteValue(transform:row1column1) * hy +
            math:AbsoluteValue(transform:row1column2) * hz
        number halfZ = math:AbsoluteValue(transform:row2column0) * hx + math:AbsoluteValue(transform:row2column1) * hy +
            math:AbsoluteValue(transform:row2column2) * hz

        minimum:Set(worldX - halfX, worldY - halfY, worldZ - halfZ)
        maximum:Set(worldX + halfX, worldY + halfY, worldZ + halfZ)
        out:Set(minimum, maximum)
    end

    /*
    This action returns true if two boxes have the same corners.
    */
    private action HasSameBounds(BoundingBox first, BoundingBox second) returns boolean
        return first:GetMinimum():GetX() = second:GetMinimum():GetX() and
            first:GetMinimum():GetY() = second:GetMinimum():GetY() and
            first:GetMinimum():GetZ() = second:GetMinimum():GetZ() and
            first:GetMaximum():GetX() = second:GetMaximum():GetX() and
            first:GetMaximum():GetY() = second:GetMaximum():GetY() and
            first:GetMaximum():GetZ() = second:GetMaximum():GetZ()
    end
end
